==English==

{{webster}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la}} {{term|scrupulus||uneasiness of mind, trouble, anxiety, doubt, scruple, literally a small sharp or pointed stone, the twenty-fourth part of an ounce}}, diminutive of {{term|scrupus||a rough or sharp stone, anxiety, uneasiness}}; perhaps akin to {{etyl|grc}} {{term|σκύρος|sc=polytonic|tr=skyros|lang=grc||the chippings of stone}}, {{term|ξυρόν|tr=ksuron|sc=polytonic||a razor}}, {{etyl|sa}} {{term|kshura||a razor}}: compare {{etyl|fr}} ''[[scrupule]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|En-us-scruple.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A weight of twenty grains; the third part of a dram.
# Hence, a very small quantity; a particle.
#: ''I will not bate thee a '''scruple'''.'' Shak.
# Hesitation as to action from the difficulty of determining what is right or expedient; unwillingness, doubt, or hesitation proceeding from motives of conscience; to consider if something is [[ethical]].
#: ''He was made miserable by the conflict between his tastes and his '''scruples'''.'' - [[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay]].
# {{obsolete}} A doubt or uncertainty concerning a matter of fact; intellectual perplexity.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|scrupl|ing}}

# {{intransitive}} To be reluctant or to hesitate, as regards an action, on account of considerations of conscience or expedience.
#: ''We are often over-precise, '''scrupling''' to say or do those things which lawfully we may.''  - [[w:Thomas Fuller|Thomas Fuller]].
#: ''Men '''scruple''' at the lawfulness of a set form of divine worship.''  - [[w:Robert South|Robert South]].
# To regard with suspicion; to hesitate at; to question.
#: ''Others long before them ... '''scrupled''' more the books of hereties than of gentiles.''  - [[w:John Milton|John Milton]].
# To excite scruples in; to cause to scruple.
#: ''Letters which did still '''scruple''' many of them.''  -E. Symmons.

